# CLAUDE.md §11's per-file ceiling, enforced instead of remembered.
#
# NOT a port. `layout.rs` walks `archive/bootstrap-rs/**/*.rs`; the compiler is
# `selfhost/**/*.hero` now — flat until M-selfhost-nesting, ten directories
# after it — and three things about it change the check itself. This is the
# rewrite panel 085 R4 said it would have to be.
#
# 1. **A Heroes module holds its own tests.** The Rust excluded whole
#    FILES — anything under a `tests/` directory, and any `tests.rs` —
#    because §11's number "governs what a reader must hold in their head,
#    and a test file is read one case at a time". In this language the
#    cases live at the bottom of the module they test, so the exclusion
#    has to be of SECTIONS rather than of files. Measured 2026-08-18, and
#    the difference is the whole check: **30 of 144** files pass 300 lines
#    counted whole, and **17** counted as code.
#
# 2. **The knot rule.** §11 yields entirely where the LANGUAGE forbids the
#    seam: Heroes refuses module cycles (`module_cycle` fires on the `use`
#    edge), and a recursive-descent grammar is mutually recursive by
#    construction — so what is five files in the Rust is one file here,
#    and no cut exists. `grammar_expr.hero` is that file.
#
# 3. **So a bare ceiling would go red on day one**, which is the shape §9
#    calls a rule nobody can obey. What stands instead is a CEILING PER
#    FILE: 300 by default, and for a file already past it the number it
#    measures today. That number is a stop on growth, not an approval —
#    the reasons are the author's to give (DECIDE.md), and until they are
#    given this check says exactly what it knows: this file is this long,
#    and it may not get longer.
#
# `tests/harness/` is excluded the way the Rust excluded `tests/`: it is
# the net, not the compiler.

use report
use shell
use strings

constant COMPILER: str
    "selfhost"

# §11's number. A threshold to think at, not a limit to round.
constant CEILING: i64
    300

# Files already past the ceiling. **A stop on growth rather than a
# licence**: a file here that grows is red, exactly as `toolchain.rs`
# reaching 425 against a decided 400 was red in the Rust.
#
# **RE-BASELINED ONCE, 2026-08-28**, when `selfhost/` became canonical:
# every file went through `heroes fmt --in-place` and `code_lines` stopped
# counting blank lines in the same commit (its own comment carries that
# reason). Both halves of the table's meaning changed at once, so every
# number was re-measured rather than nudged — and the table is the same
# SIZE it was, 16 entries, with five files SMALLER than before and two
# (`cursor.hero`, `source.hero`) dropping out entirely.
#
# This is a re-baseline and not a raise, and the difference is worth
# stating because it is the only thing that keeps the ratchet honest: a
# raise hides the growth of a file somebody edited, and every number here
# moved because the metric and the layout both changed underneath all 165
# files at once. It happens exactly once. From the next commit on, a file
# here that grows is red again.
#
# The one number that WAS raised, four times on 2026-08-26 and -28:
# `print_fmt.hero` 843 -> 888 -> 903 -> 996 -> 1005 for defect 003 (docs/work/DONE.md),
# defect 004 (docs/work/DONE.md), panel 095 stage 3 and WIDTH 88 -> 120. Raised, never
# split, because the language refuses the only cut available — 003's two
# functions read `power` and `wrapped`, so the seam runs print_bodies ->
# print_fmt -> print_bodies and `module_cycle` fires on the `use` edge
# (§11's knot clause).
#
# **It stands at 1150, a round number, by author decision 2026-08-28**
# (`/decide`, answer `7c`), and the open question it was keeping is
# retired. The argument FOR splitting was real and is recorded here rather
# than lost: four of the five defects found in that module across three
# days were one class — a measurement or a bookkeeping fact that
# disagreed with what the printer prints. The author took the counter:
# those repairs made the module sounder, so splitting now is risk with no
# defect asking for it.
#
# The author chose **1100** against a measured 1083, and the same
# session's own repairs took the file to **1106** before the commit landed
# — the comments explaining `field_line`'s §4.1 discriminator and the
# parser guard. So the ceiling is **1150**: it honours a decision taken
# twenty minutes earlier with room in it, rather than asking the same
# question again over six lines of comment. `grammar_expr.hero` went
# 954 -> **1000** in the same commit, for `ends_the_expression` and the
# paragraph naming panel 007's Amendment B as the rule it enforces.
#
# **Three numbers moved on 2026-09-02, for a ratified language feature and
# not for creep** (panels 099 and 100: `use` paths, then `as` and per-file
# uniqueness). `ast.hero` 449 -> **455** for one field on `record Use` plus
# the two lines that say why it is total and not an `Option` — an AST is
# mutually recursive by construction, so §11's knot clause applies and there
# is no cut. `resolve_state.hero` 308 -> **315** for a did-you-mean list that
# now offers local names. `parse_use.hero` is a NEW entry at **310** against
# §11's 300: that file is one declaration and its five refusals, and the
# refusals are its stated subject rather than an appendix, so the only cut
# available would move the message away from the cursor that recovers from it.
#
# **A fifth moved the same day, for a DEFECT repair and not a feature**
# (defect 028). `emit/ctype.hero` 370 -> **380**, measured 371: one line, the
# `seen` map that `synth.collect` needs now that it walks a record's fields
# rather than returning at `.named`. The guard exists because a record may hold
# itself through `[T]` or `{K: V}` (spec § 3), so the walk has a cycle in it,
# and it is a SECOND set rather than the existing one because `into` is the
# list of function typedefs to emit and a named type in it would emit a typedef
# for a record. Raised with room, as every entry here is, rather than to the
# measured line.
#
# **Four moved on 2026-09-12, for a ratified surface form, and the number of
# files is the story** (panel 132: `Point::x`, the field-name operator). The
# form is one AST node, and a new node is a new arm in every exhaustive
# `match` over `ExprKind` — **68 of them**, which the compiler listed one by
# one and would not let a session forget. Four of the files holding those arms
# were already over §11's 300, so four ceilings move: `ast.hero` 491 ->
# **505** (measured 500), `check/walk.hero` 1842 -> **1870** (measured 1859,
# and it carries the typing arm and the near-miss diagnostic as well as the
# enumeration), `grammar_expr.hero` 1044 -> **1085** (measured 1075, the `::`
# postfix and its own parser) and `ir/flatten.hero` 1123 -> **1150** (measured
# 1139, where the form is ERASED to a literal, which is what makes it sugar).
#
# **The cost is the author's choice and the record says so rather than
# rounding it.** A prototype spelled `Point.x` reused the existing field node
# and cost +59 code lines in three files with no ceiling moved; the author
# took `::` against it, because `.` already qualifies a module and already
# reads a field from a value, and a third meaning would make the line legible
# only to a reader who knows whether the base names a type or a value. `::`
# has to be in the tree for the formatter to re-print it, so it is its own
# node, and its own node is these four numbers.
#
# Each is raised with room rather than to the measured line, which is the
# lesson `print_fmt.hero` paid for twice: a ceiling set at today's exact count
# asks the same question again over six lines of comment. If panel 101 removes
# the `as` clause, these fall on their own and the table should be re-measured
# rather than left generous.
#
# **A fourth moved the same day, for a ratified REFUSAL rather than a feature**
# (panel 102, ratified: a local may not take a module's name).
# `resolve_state.hero` 315 -> **330** against a measured 325, for a fourth arm
# on `declare`, which already had three of the same shape — a name refused
# because another local holds it, because a built-in does, because a top-level
# declaration does, and now because a `use` bound it. **The seat that priced
# the change made this argument rather than the knot argument**, and it also
# corrected the brief: this file's own module doc says it is one module
# *"WITHOUT a cycle forcing it"*, so §11's language-forbids-the-seam clause does
# not reach it. What reaches it is the plainer half — a reader holding the first
# three arms holds the fourth for free, which is what the ~300 protects and what
# this addition does not spend. The whole diagnostic is 8 lines of comment and
# 3 of code; its argument lives on `docs/panel/102` instead, which is CLAUDE.md's
# preamble applied to a comment for once.
#
# **Two moved on 2026-09-03, M-robustness-guards step 1** (`@` on an
# immutable binding accepted and corrupting memory; `@bs[0]` on a cell
# handed the callee the whole array). `ir/lower.hero` 1483 -> **1511**:
# `lower_args` and `with_receiver` hand an `@` argument through an index to
# the new `ir/inout.hero` and `emit_call` pays the write-back after the
# instruction — 14 of the 28 lines are the formatter breaking two
# `emit_call` sites whose argument list grew by one word. The mechanism
# itself is 145 lines in its own module, which is the knot argument applied
# in the right direction: the ring gained a hand-over, not a feature.
# `grammar_expr.hero` 1000 -> **1002**: `@` before something that is not a
# place is refused where `1 @ 2` already is, six lines of code beside
# `is_place`, which is the function that decides both.
#
# **Four moved on 2026-09-09 for panel 121, M-interpolated-strings, and the
# sitting predicted the first of them.** The `f"…"` literal is ONE AST node with
# its holes as children (R4), and R5 measured that a helper module which calls
# `parse_expr` closes a `use` cycle — so the hook that reads the holes stays in
# the grammar's knot, which is the case CLAUDE.md §11's knot clause exists for.
# `grammar_expr.hero` 1002 -> **1041**: `interpolation`, the loop that parses
# each hole between two pieces, with its comment (17); `interp_render`, the
# test section's fully-parenthesised re-print, which calls `render` and so
# cannot leave the file either (15); one `use`, the hook in `primary`, one
# token in the operator table and three arms joining a literal group. The
# compiler seat's prediction at panel 121 — *strictly greater than 1002* — is
# confirmed by this row. `check/walk.hero` 1700 -> **1708**: the typing arm,
# which walks each hole by recursion only this knot can do and hands the type
# to `check/interp.hero` to judge; one `use`. `ir/flatten.hero` 1110 -> **1114**:
# the lowering arm, which lowers each hole by the recursion only this knot can
# do and hands the values to `ir/interp.hero` for the concatenation; one `use`.
# `ast.hero` 475 -> **484**: the node itself with its comment, and `holes_depth`
# beside the test section's `depth`, which is where the tree's own tests
# measure nesting. The three helper modules — `lex_interp.hero`, `check/interp.hero`,
# `ir/interp.hero` — hold everything that needed no call back into a knot.
#
# **Two moved on 2026-09-03 for panel 105, ratified the evening it sat**: a
# type parameter the arguments leave unbound takes the type the context asks
# for. `check/walk.hero` 1583 -> **1700** against a measured 1664: `check()`'s
# `.call` arm keeps its expectation instead of falling to `synth`, the
# `.method` arm gets one, four call-dispatch functions carry `expected: i64?`,
# and `user_call` binds from the context and reports what nothing bound — all
# inside the checker's knot (check/synth/block/statement are mutually
# recursive; §11's knot clause), and the paragraphs that say why are the
# larger part of the growth, by CLAUDE.md §11's own rule that a comment
# teaches the invariant. `ir/mono.hero` 367 -> **380** against a measured 368:
# one field, `instantiated_at`, so the emitter's gate can report an instance
# at the call that made it (the pragmatist's blocking condition 4). Raised
# with room, as the precedents above were.
# **Three moved on 2026-09-04, panel 106's repair** — the exit sweep stops
# minting a load temporary per swept slot per returning block, which was 58.5%
# of every declared frame in the emitted C. All three raises are the SAME
# addition seen from three files, and in every one of them the code is small
# and the comment is the reason: an op that carries a slot rather than a value
# is a departure from what `ir.hero` had asserted for a year — *releasing a
# slot IS a load followed by a decref, which keeps one form instead of two* —
# so the file that made the claim is the file that has to withdraw it, in
# writing, with the measurement. `ir.hero` is a NEW entry at **310** against a
# measured 305: 4 lines of case and the paragraph that says why the old
# sentence was true and expensive. `emit/inst.hero` 324 -> **350** against a
# measured 343: the whole change there is one emitted arm plus the split of
# `reference` into a value door and a name door, which exists so that the two
# ways to reach a refcount call cannot learn a type apart. `ir/print.hero`
# 460 -> **470** against a measured 465: one line prints the slot by name, and
# four say why printing it as a decref of an invented temporary would make
# `--dump-ir` describe a program the emitter no longer writes.
# **One moved on 2026-09-04, defect 012's repair**: `emit/gate.hero` 341 ->
# **365** against a measured 355. `function f(_: ())` reached clang as
# `int64_t h_f(void h0_)` — *argument may not have 'void' type*, exit 2, the
# compiler blaming itself — and the FIELD position of that same hole was
# closed 2026-08-12, twelve lines below, by `unit_fields`. `_` is the one
# spelling that got through: a named `()` parameter is `unused_binding` when
# it is not read and `bound_unit` when it is, and `_` binds nothing so
# neither rule fires. Five lines of check inside the loop that already walks
# `f.params`, one route in `what_the_author_can_do`, and the comment that
# says which sibling this is — the ceiling is what forced the count to be
# read at all, which is the point of pinning it. Raised with room, as the
# precedents above were.
#
# **TWO ROWS LEFT AND ONE ARRIVED ON 2026-09-06, M-declared-freer step 1**, and
# the reason is a seat's condition rather than tidying: panel 109's `owned <C
# function>` mark lands in both of those files and neither had a line of room.
# `selfhost/ir/lower.hero` **1511 -> 174** and `selfhost/parse/decl.hero`
# **638 -> 217**, both now under §11's own 300 and out of this table; the one
# arrival is `selfhost/ir/flatten.hero` at **1110** against a measured 1104.
#
# **The two cuts are not the same kind of cut, and the difference is the
# finding.** `parse/decl.hero` had NO knot — its call graph is a DAG — and the
# ring its own module doc called unsplittable (*"one module because the
# language leaves no choice"*) was a fact about where the shared tails LIVED,
# never about the grammar. `file -> extern_group -> members -> constant_tail`
# closes only while the tails sit beside the dispatcher. Moved into
# `parse/tails.hero`, which neither of the other two is used by, the edges run
# one way: **217 + 246 + 235**, no entry needed for any of the three. The
# answer had been printing itself in `module_cycle`'s own note the whole time
# — *"move what both modules need into a third one that neither uses"*.
#
# `ir/lower.hero` DOES have a knot, and it is why `flatten.hero` needs a row at
# all: **25 functions, 990 code lines** by this file's own counting rule, all
# reaching each other through `expr`. The declaration walk is the one part
# that only ever calls INWARDS, so it stays behind as `ir/lower.hero` at 174
# and keeps the name — which is why the **21** modules calling `lower.lower(…)`
# did not move a character. What else could leave left by a mechanical rule
# rather than a line count: `ir/questions.hero` (**149**) is everything with no
# `@b` parameter, `ir/emissions.hero` (**185**) everything that emits one fixed
# shape and never walks back into the tree.
#
# **That the knot cannot be cut is measured, not assumed** (CLAUDE.md §1: a
# failed search is not an impossibility). The one route out of a mutually
# recursive walk is to hand the recursion over as a function value, and this
# walk threads its builder as `@b`: `(function(@Box) -> ())` is
# `error[expected_type]: expected a type, found @`, so no such value exists to
# hand over. Panel 109's coordinator predicted `ir/lower.hero` at **≤ 900**;
# the entry landed at 174 and the knot beside it at 1104, and that prediction
# is scored FALSE in the panel file with this number rather than argued around.
# **`ast.hero` 455 -> 475 on 2026-09-06, M-declared-freer step 2**, against a
# measured 466 and raised with room as every precedent above was. Two fields
# for panel 109's ratified `owned <C function>`: `FunctionDecl.owned_result`
# and `Param.owned`, each a `token.Span?` holding the freer's NAME so a
# diagnostic about it can point at the word the author wrote. **The knot clause
# is what applies here and it applies without argument** — an AST is mutually
# recursive by construction, `Expr` through `Stmt` through `Block` and back, so
# no cut exists and the file is one module or nothing (this is the same row
# panels 099 and 100 raised for `use` paths, for the same reason). **The code
# is 2 lines and the comments are 13**, which is the ratio §11 asks for: each
# field's comment says why it rides where it does — the result's because a
# diagnostic needs the span, the parameter's because two parameters of one
# signature can be owned by different functions and because the cell is
# out-only.
# **One moved on 2026-09-11 for panel 128, and the seat that priced it predicted
# the number before it built anything.** A label at a call through a function
# value is refused now, six diff lines in `check/walk.hero`, and `heroes fmt`
# broke the call across three: 1746 -> **1751**, which is the seat's own
# prediction 2 scored on the instrument. Both places moved together this time,
# the table and the assert below, because the day before they did not and the CI
# went red on all three legs.
#
# **Five moved on 2026-09-11 for panel 127, defect 025, and every one of them
# moved by a measured number rather than a chosen one.** The label rule reached
# the two built-ins whose parameters share a type, so 1022 positional `fail(`
# calls across the tree gained `code:` and `msg:`, and `heroes fmt` broke the
# lines that passed its 120 columns: `check/walk.hero` 1708 -> **1746**, which
# is the five call-site lines and the two helpers the certainty of a
# `wrong_label` fix now asks for, plus the wraps; `ir/flatten.hero` 1114 ->
# **1123**; `grammar_expr.hero` 1041 -> **1044**; `check/builtins.hero` 374 ->
# **377**; `emit/ctype.hero` 365 -> **370**, which also lost a malformed code,
# `"compiler bug"` becoming `compiler_bug`, found by the sitting's historian
# predicting that one of the 1022 sites would be malformed. The compiler seat
# predicted ten rows would move and the instrument says five: the wraps landed
# in fewer files than the estimate, and the estimate is in `docs/panel/127-the-rule-that-did-not-reach-its-own-library.md`
# beside this sentence so the gap is on the record rather than tidied away.
#
# **Two more moved on 2026-09-11, hours later, for M-named-callbacks**, the route
# the author chose over panel 129's own recommendation. `check/table.hero` 371 ->
# **389**: `fits` gained its second blank-filling case, a type carrying NO run of
# names at all, without which a written type naming a position nothing can
# confuse was uninhabited by any function in the language — panel 130's
# spec-warden built its own compiler and ran the program rather than reading for
# it. `selfhost/value_errors.hero` **334**, a new row: one diagnostic,
# `callback_name`, with the note and the paragraph that says why no fix is
# offered. It is not split, and the reason is the one §11 gives for a knot — a
# diagnostics module is one concern read one message at a time, and cutting it by
# line count would put two messages of one family in two files.
#
# **Three moved on 2026-09-11 for defect 026's repair**, the conservative route
# panel 128 priced and scheduled: a function type carries its parameter names,
# mandatory where two of them share a type, and the names are part of the type's
# identity. The compiler seat's prediction 2 was written before anything was
# built — *the net names both `check/walk.hero` and `ast.hero`, two or more
# ceilings rise, and walk exceeds 1780* — and the instrument says 1842, so it
# HELD on all three counts and named one file it did not: `check/table.hero`.
# The numbers below are the ones measured AFTER the sitting, because three of
# the five seats sent the work back: the labels became per position, `_` stopped
# being a name, and a name used twice became an error.
#
# `check/walk.hero` 1751 -> **1842**: the call made through a function value now
# asks §4.9's two questions instead of refusing every label, a declared function
# carries its parameter names into its value type at the positions that need
# them, and the two helpers those need. `ast.hero` 484 -> **491**: one field on the function-type node, with the
# comment saying why the parser records the names and judges only their count.
# `check/table.hero` **371**, a new row rather than a raised one: the names live
# in an arena beside the parameter run, keyed the same way, the file gained the
# one relation the checker has — `fits`, which is an id comparison in every case
# but a parameter a declaration called `_` — and it passes 300 for the first
# time. It is not split, and the reason is the one
# `.claude/rules/module-shape.md` already ratifies for a 400-line module — the
# cut would run straight through the shared canonical key, which is the single
# thing this file exists to own, and two modules that both had to agree on
# `ty_key` would be one module wearing two names.
#
constant DECIDED: [str]
    [
        "selfhost/check/walk.hero 1870"
        "selfhost/print/fmt.hero 1150"
        "selfhost/ir/flatten.hero 1150"
        "selfhost/grammar_expr.hero 1085"
        "selfhost/ir/print.hero 470"
        "selfhost/ast.hero 505"
        "selfhost/check/table.hero 389"
        "selfhost/value_errors.hero 334"
        "selfhost/check/builtins.hero 377"
        "selfhost/ir/mono.hero 380"
        "selfhost/emit/ctype.hero 380"
        "selfhost/emit/gate.hero 365"
        "selfhost/resolve/cycles.hero 331"
        "selfhost/resolve/state.hero 330"
        "selfhost/emit/inst.hero 350"
        "selfhost/ir.hero 310"
        "selfhost/modules.hero 320"
        "selfhost/parse/use_line.hero 310"
    ]

# The floor is the Rust's own idea —
# `assert!(files.len() > 100, "the walk is not reaching the tree")` — and
# it exists because a walk that finds nothing reports green.
#
# **Set at 140 against 144 modules on 2026-08-18. Re-measured 2026-09-12: 202
# modules, so the slack is 62 rather than 4.** The number is left alone here
# deliberately — what a floor owes as the tree grows is a question for the panel
# and not for this line — but the measurement is written down, because the
# paragraph below reasons from the old one. A floor's job is to catch a PARTIAL
# walk, and a partial walk of a bigger tree returns a bigger number: what was a
# net with four lines of give now has sixty-two.
constant LEAST: i64
    140

function run_all(scratch: str) -> report.Report?
    r: report.Report @ report.empty()

    # **`list_tree`, not `list_entries`, and the difference is 133 files.**
    # `list_entries` scans with `recursive: 0`, which was the whole tree until
    # M-selfhost-nesting put 133 of 170 modules into ten directories. The
    # ratchet would then have measured the 37 that stayed flat — including
    # none of the four largest files in the table below. It does not fail
    # quietly, because `LEAST` is a floor on the COUNT and 37 is under it, and
    # that floor is the Rust's own idea for exactly this reason. But a check
    # that survives a layout change by going red is a check somebody has to
    # repair under pressure; this one recurses instead.
    #
    # **That last argument is still true and it is no longer safe, 2026-09-12.**
    # It compares 37 against a floor of 140, and both numbers are from a tree of
    # 144 modules. The tree is 202 now: the same flattening today would leave
    # the 37 that never moved, which is still under the floor, but the margin is
    # luck rather than design — a partial walk returning 150 of 202 passes, and
    # that is three quarters of the compiler going unmeasured in green.
    nothing: [str] = []
    files = shell.list_tree(scratch: scratch, dir: COMPILER, skip: nothing)?
    modules: [str] @ []

    for path in files
        if strings.ends_with(text: path, suffix: ".hero")
            modules @ modules.push(path)

    if modules.len() < LEAST
        report.keep_failure(
            @r,
            label: "layout",
            detail: "  the walk found " + modules.len().to_str() + " modules and had " + LEAST.to_str() + " — it is not reaching the tree"
        )
    over: [str] @ []

    for path in modules
        text = read_file(path)

        if text.is_err()
            report.keep_failure(@r, label: "layout/" + strings.base_name(path: path), detail: "  cannot read " + path)
            continue
        lines = code_lines(text.must())
        limit = ceiling_for(path)

        if lines > limit
            if limit == CEILING
                over @ over.push(
                    "    " + path + ": " + lines.to_str() + " lines of code, past §11's " + CEILING.to_str()
                )
            else
                over @ over.push(
                    "    " + path + ": " + lines.to_str() + " lines of code, past the " + limit.to_str() + " it measured when this check was written — a file already over the ceiling may not grow further"
                )

    if over.len() > 0
        report.keep_failure(
            @r,
            label: "layout",
            detail: "  CLAUDE.md §11: *split a module before it passes ~300 lines*, because \"the author must be able to open any file and read it without drowning\":\n" + over.join("\n") + "\n  Split along a seam that names a concern, never at a line count. Where the LANGUAGE forbids the seam — a knot of mutually recursive functions, which cannot be two modules because Heroes refuses module cycles — the file stays and its module doc owes a map: the cycle, the entry points, which function calls which."
        )
    else
        report.keep_pass(@r)
    return ok(r)

# The lines a reader must hold in their head: everything outside a `test`
# block, AND NOT A BLANK LINE.
#
# A `test "..."` at column 0 opens one, and the block ends at the next
# line that starts at column 0 and is not a comment — which is the same
# boundary the language uses, since a block is what is indented under its
# header.
#
# BLANK LINES STOPPED COUNTING ON 2026-08-28, and the reason is §11's own
# sentence: this number governs *"what a reader must hold in their head"*,
# and a blank line is the one line nobody holds — it is what makes the
# others holdable. Counting them was harmless while the formatter only
# preserved the author's, and became perverse the day panel 095 had it
# INSERT them: the ceiling would have punished exactly the change made to
# make these files easier to read. Measured the day it changed, over the
# swept `selfhost/`: **36 files past their ceiling counting blanks, 10
# without**, and the re-baselined table is the same SIZE as the old one
# (16 entries) with **five files measuring SMALLER than before** —
# `ast.hero` 495 -> 449, `ir_print.hero` 485 -> 460, `resolve_state.hero`
# 338 -> 308 — and two, `cursor.hero` and `source.hero`, leaving the
# table entirely. That is what says the metric change is not a way to
# hide growth: it moves numbers in both directions.
function code_lines(text: str) -> i64
    count: i64 @ 0
    inside: bool @ false

    for row in strings.lines(text)
        if strings.starts_with(text: row, prefix: "test \"")
            inside @ true
            continue

        if inside && row.len() > 0 && !starts_indented(row) && !strings.starts_with(text: row, prefix: "#")
            inside @ false

        if !inside && strings.trimmed(row).len() > 0
            count @ count + 1

    return count

function starts_indented(row: str) -> bool
    return row[0] == 32 || row[0] == 9

# The ceiling this file is held to: its DECIDED number, or §11's.
function ceiling_for(path: str) -> i64
    for entry in DECIDED
        parts = strings.words(entry)

        if parts.len() == 2 && parts[0] == path
            number = strings.to_number(parts[1])

            if !number.is_err()
                return number.must()

    return CEILING

test "code_lines counts what a reader holds and not the cases"
    program = "function f() -> i64\n    return 1\n\ntest \"one\"\n    assert f() == 1\n    assert true\n\nfunction g() -> i64\n    return 2\n"

    # FOUR: the two functions' four lines and nothing else. It was five
    # until 2026-08-28, the fifth being the blank line before the test —
    # blank lines stopped counting when the formatter started inserting
    # them, and the reason is on `code_lines` itself. What the old comment
    # said about the blank AFTER the test is still true and still the
    # reason this is asserted rather than assumed: a blank line does not
    # close a block, so that one belongs to the case either way.
    assert code_lines(program) == 4
    assert code_lines("function f()\n    print(1)\n") == 2
    assert code_lines("test \"only a test\"\n    assert true\n") == 0

    # And the property the change is FOR, asserted directly: air inside a
    # body costs a reader nothing, so it costs this count nothing.
    dense = "function f() -> i64\n    a = 1\n    b = 2\n    return a + b\n"
    airy = "function f() -> i64\n    a = 1\n\n    b = 2\n\n    return a + b\n"
    assert code_lines(dense) == code_lines(airy)

test "a comment inside a test block does not close it"
    program = "test \"one\"\n    assert true\n# a comment at column 0, still inside the case's paragraph\n    assert true\nfunction after()\n    print(1)\n"
    assert code_lines(program) == 2

test "the ceiling is per file, and a decided file keeps its own"
    # **THE WITNESSES WERE TWO INCISED NUMBERS UNTIL 2026-09-12, AND THEY ARE
    # THE WHOLE TABLE NOW.** What this case has to catch is a lookup that
    # quietly returns CEILING for a file that IS in the table. Two hand-picked
    # rows caught that for two files and cost a hand-edit at every raise; the
    # property below catches it for all eighteen and costs none, because it
    # rests on something the table cannot stop being true of itself:
    #
    #   **every row of DECIDED is above CEILING, by construction.** A file at
    #   or under §11's own 300 has no reason to be in the table — that is the
    #   condition under which a row LEAVES it, and two rows left that way on
    #   2026-09-06 at 174 and 217 (below). So `> CEILING` for every row is a
    #   fact about the table's meaning rather than about today's files, and a
    #   lookup falling through to the fallback fails it on the first row.
    #
    # It catches strictly more than the two numbers did. A row somebody
    # mistypes into three words, or whose number stops parsing, now fails here
    # as well — `ceiling_for` returns CEILING for such a row, silently, and no
    # assert reached that before.
    #
    # What is NOT weakened: the paragraphs below stay, because they are what
    # this case cost to learn, and `DECIDED.len()` stays incised at the bottom
    # for the opposite reason — that number is not a duplicate of the table,
    # it is the notice that a row arrived or left.
    #
    # **AND THEY DID NOT, FOR SIX COMMITS.** This assert read `resolve_state`
    # at 308 while the table said 315, so `heroes test tests/harness/main.hero`
    # was 92/1 from `9599d97` to `d08062f` — and that commit's own diff says
    # what it was doing, in the comment above DECIDED: *"`resolve_state.hero`
    # 308 -> 315 ... `parse_use.hero` is a NEW entry at 310"*. The knowledge was
    # written down two screens up. What was missing is a caller: this suite is
    # in `docs/ROADMAP.md`'s verify block and was in neither CLAUDE.md
    # § Commands nor `/step`'s close checklist, both of which named two suites.
    # It is in both now, which is the actual repair — the numbers below are only
    # the symptom. `DESIGN-LOG` had recorded the same failure on 2026-08-31
    # (*"a suite that had never been run"*, 83 of 90) and left it without one.
    #
    # The witness is now the entry nearest CEILING **by lookup rather than by
    # name**, which is what the sentence above always claimed it was.
    #
    # **AND THIS LINE IS WHY THE NUMBER WAS WRITTEN TWICE, paid for on
    # 2026-09-11.** Panel 127 raised five DECIDED rows and this assert kept the
    # old one, so the table said 1746 and the test said 1708. Every suite of the
    # net was green and the CI went red on all three legs, because the net's own
    # tests are the THIRD suite and the session had run the other two after its
    # last edit. That is CL-054 exactly: run the suite you did not expect to
    # move. The number stayed in two places on purpose — one the ceiling, the
    # other the proof that the lookup reads it — and the cost of forgetting the
    # second was three red legs rather than a silent pass. It was paid twice:
    # 1842 -> 1870 on 2026-09-12 (panel 132's field-name operator) moved the
    # table and this line with it.
    #
    # **The third time is what retired the duplication, 2026-09-12.** The
    # defence above is sound about WHAT the second copy proves and says nothing
    # about the form it has to take: the proof that the lookup reads the table
    # can be read OUT of the table, and then no raise can forget it. Twice is
    # the count that made the argument — a rule whose obedience costs a manual
    # edit at every use is a rule that will be missed, and this one was, at the
    # only two raises it has ever seen.
    for entry in DECIDED
        parts = strings.words(entry)

        # The row is well formed, which nothing asserted before: a row of
        # three words falls through `ceiling_for` to CEILING in silence.
        assert parts.len() == 2
        number = strings.to_number(parts[1])

        assert !number.is_err()

        # The property itself. A lookup that returns the fallback for a row
        # that is IN the table fails here, on that row.
        assert number.must() > CEILING
        assert ceiling_for(parts[0]) == number.must()

    assert ceiling_for("selfhost/anything-else.hero") == CEILING

    # **16 since M-package-layout, and the direction is the point.** The table
    # shrinks when a file is FIXED: `cli_verbs.hero` was in it at 358, the
    # milestone split the produce pipeline out to `cli_produce.hero`, and 207
    # code lines needs no entry — that took it to 15. A row leaving is the
    # ratchet working. A row arriving is a file nobody could cut, and that is
    # the author's to allow: `parse_use.hero` arrived at 310 in `9599d97`, when
    # `use` learned to be a path.
    #
    # This count exists so neither direction happens quietly — it went red the
    # moment the row was removed, which is exactly the notice it owes.
    #
    # **It was also red for six commits and nobody saw it**, because the assert
    # above it fails first and Heroes stops a case at its first failed assert.
    # One stale number hid a second one. Two lessons and both are cheap: a case
    # that pins several independent facts hides all but the first, and the
    # suite that holds it needs a caller (see above).
    #
    # **16 -> 17 on 2026-09-04**, `selfhost/ir.hero` at 310: panel 106's repair
    # adds an `OpKind` case that carries a slot rather than a value, and the
    # paragraph withdrawing what that file had asserted for a year is longer
    # than the case. The count moved because a row was ADDED, which is the
    # direction this assert notices least loudly — a removal takes the table
    # below the number and a raise leaves it alone, so an addition is the one
    # that has to be written down here on purpose.
    #
    # **17 -> 16 on 2026-09-06**, M-declared-freer step 1, and it is the good
    # direction twice over: `ir/lower.hero` and `parse/decl.hero` were CUT out
    # of this table — 1511 -> 174 and 638 -> 217, both under §11's own 300 —
    # and only one row arrived, `ir/flatten.hero`, which is the knot the
    # language refuses to split. Two rows out and one in is a net of minus one,
    # and the ceiling that was the highest in the lowering fell from 1511 to
    # 1110. The reason for each number is above DECIDED.
    assert DECIDED.len() == 18
